1	# Mijia MCP Server
2	
3	[中文文档](README.md) | English
4	
5	A productized Mijia MCP server built on top of `mijiaAPI 3.x`. Clients do not need to understand `did`, `siid/piid/aiid`, or other low-level protocol details up front. The server is designed around homes, rooms, device names, and scene names first.
6	
7	## What This Version Solves
8	
9	- For AI clients: exposes stable product-level tools instead of raw protocol fields
10	- For real smart-home workflows: browse homes and rooms first, then resolve devices, then control them
11	- For MCP compatibility: tools return structured results, and service/login state is directly consumable
12	- For future growth: standard capability schemas, profile-driven controls, and resource models can keep evolving
13	
14	## Current Capabilities
15	
16	### Service and Login
17	- `get_service_status`
18	- `prepare_login`
19	- `reconnect_service`
20	- `clear_saved_login`
21	- `refresh_devices`
22	- `get_tool_catalog`
23	- `ping`
24	
25	### Homes and Devices
26	- `get_home_overview`
27	- `list_homes`
28	- `list_devices`
29	- `get_device`
30	- `get_device_status`
31	- `get_device_capabilities`
32	
33	### Device Control
34	- `control_by_intent`
35	- `control_device`
36	- `turn_on_device`
37	- `turn_off_device`
38	- `set_brightness`
39	- `set_color_temperature`
40	- `set_target_temperature`
41	- `set_hvac_mode`
42	- `set_fan_speed`
43	- `set_cover_position`
44	
45	### Scenes and Consumables
46	- `list_scenes`
47	- `execute_scene`
48	- `get_consumable_items`
49	
50	### MCP Resources
51	- `mijia://service`
52	- `mijia://homes`
53	- `mijia://devices`
54	- `mijia://scenes`
55	- `mijia://capabilities`
56	- `mijia://tooling`
57	
58	## Installation
59	
60	Python `3.10+` is recommended.
61	
62	```bash
63	poetry install
64	```
65	
66	If you do not use Poetry:
67	
68	```bash
69	pip install -r requirements.txt
70	```
71	
72	## Start the Server
73	
74	```bash
75	poetry run python mcp_server/mcp_server.py
76	```
77	
78	Test the MCP handshake:
79	
80	```bash
81	poetry run python mcp_server/mcp_test.py
82	```
83	
84	## Authentication
85	
86	`mijiaAPI 3.x` removed username/password login. QR login is the only supported path.
87	
88	When login is needed, the server will:
89	
90	- generate a browser-friendly page at `~/.miot-mcp/qr.html`
91	- also generate a QR image at `~/.miot-mcp/qr.png`
92	- open `qr.html` in the default browser by default
93	- only fall back to the image viewer or terminal QR when browser open is unavailable
94	
95	Credentials are stored in:
96	
97	```text
98	~/.miot-mcp/auth_data.json
99	```
100	
101	### Recommended Login Flow
102	
103	1. Call `prepare_login`
104	2. Call `get_service_status`
105	3. Read `service.qr.page_path` or `service.qr.image_path`
106	4. After scanning, call `reconnect_service` or go straight to `refresh_devices`
107	
108	### Login State Fields
109	
110	Both `get_service_status` and `mijia://service` return structured login state. The most useful fields are:
111	
112	- `service.connected`
113	- `service.has_saved_login`
114	- `service.qr.open_mode`
115	- `service.qr.page_path`
116	- `service.qr.image_path`
117	- `service.qr.login_url`
118	- `assistant_summary`
119	- `next_steps.should_scan_qr`
120	
121	## Environment Variables
122	
123	```bash
124	export MIJIA_ENABLE_QR="true"
125	export MIJIA_QR_OPEN_MODE="browser"
126	export MIJIA_LOG_LEVEL="INFO"
127	```
128	
129	Notes:
130	
131	- `MIJIA_ENABLE_QR`: whether QR login is enabled, defaults to `true`
132	- `MIJIA_QR_OPEN_MODE`: advanced override for QR opening, supports `browser`, `viewer`, or `none`
133	- `MIJIA_LOG_LEVEL`: one of `DEBUG`, `INFO`, `WARNING`, `ERROR`
134	
135	## MCP Client Configuration Example
136	
137	Using the virtualenv Python directly is recommended over `poetry run`.
138	
139	```json
140	{
141	  "mcpServers": {
142	    "mijia": {
143	      "command": "/path/to/venv/bin/python",
144	      "args": [
145	        "/path/to/miot-mcp/mcp_server/mcp_server.py"
146	      ],
147	      "env": {
148	        "MIJIA_ENABLE_QR": "true",
149	        "MIJIA_QR_OPEN_MODE": "browser",
150	        "MIJIA_LOG_LEVEL": "INFO"
151	      }
152	    }
153	  }
154	}
155	```
156	
157	## Recommended Tool Flow
158	
159	For most AI clients, the recommended path is:
160	
161	1. `prepare_login`
162	2. `get_service_status`
163	3. `refresh_devices`
164	4. `get_home_overview`
165	5. `get_device_status`
166	6. `control_by_intent`
167	7. `list_scenes`
168	8. `execute_scene`
169	
170	If the client needs more explicit and stable routing, add:
171	
172	1. `list_homes`
173	2. `list_devices`
174	3. `get_device`
175	4. `get_device_capabilities`
176	5. `control_device`
177	
178	## Common Tools
179	
180	### `prepare_login`
181	
182	Prepares QR login proactively. By default it reuses existing QR artifacts when possible. If you need a fresh QR login flow, pass `force_reauth=true`.
183	
184	### `get_service_status`
185	
186	Returns service connection state, auth file path, log path, QR paths, and next-step hints.
187	
188	### `get_home_overview`
189	
190	Returns a room-first overview of homes and devices. This is usually the best way for a client to understand the household layout.
191	
192	### `get_device_status`
193	
194	Returns the current state of a device, supported operations, and recommended next actions.
195	
196	### `get_device_capabilities`
197	
198	Returns the standard capability schema and profile-driven controls for stable client routing.
199	
200	### `control_by_intent`
201	
202	Natural-language-like control entry point. Best for most everyday interactions, such as "set the bedroom lamp brightness to 30%".
203	
204	### `control_device`
205	
206	Structured control entry point. Best when the client already knows the target operation and arguments.
207	
208	## Examples
209	
210	### Get Service Status
211	
212	```json
213	{
214	  "name": "get_service_status",
215	  "arguments": {}
216	}
217	```
218	
219	### Prepare Login
220	
221	```json
222	{
223	  "name": "prepare_login",
224	  "arguments": {
225	    "reopen_qr": true
226	  }
227	}
228	```
229	
230	### Refresh Device and Room Mapping
231	
232	```json
233	{
234	  "name": "refresh_devices",
235	  "arguments": {}
236	}
237	```
238	
239	### Get Home Overview
240	
241	```json
242	{
243	  "name": "get_home_overview",
244	  "arguments": {}
245	}
246	```
247	
248	### Get Device Status
249	
250	```json
251	{
252	  "name": "get_device_status",
253	  "arguments": {
254	    "device_name": "Ceiling Light",
255	    "room": "Living Room"
256	  }
257	}
258	```
259	
260	### Get Capability Schema
261	
262	```json
263	{
264	  "name": "get_device_capabilities",
265	  "arguments": {
266	    "device_name": "Desk Lamp",
267	    "room": "Bedroom"
268	  }
269	}
270	```
271	
272	### Natural Language Control
273	
274	```json
275	{
276	  "name": "control_by_intent",
277	  "arguments": {
278	    "query": "Set the bedroom lamp brightness to 30%"
279	  }
280	}
281	```
282	
283	### Structured Control
284	
285	```json
286	{
287	  "name": "control_device",
288	  "arguments": {
289	    "operation": "set_color_temperature",
290	    "device_name": "Desk Lamp",
291	    "room": "Bedroom",
292	    "value": 4000
293	  }
294	}
295	```
296	
297	### Execute a Scene
298	
299	```json
300	{
301	  "name": "execute_scene",
302	  "arguments": {
303	    "scene_name": "Coming Home"
304	  }
305	}
306	```
307	
308	## Current Scope
309	
310	This version focuses on the most common home-control workflows:
311	
312	- browsing homes and rooms
313	- resolving devices
314	- controlling common standard capabilities
315	- exposing standardized capability schemas
316	- executing scenes
317	- reading consumable items
318	
319	The most important capability families currently covered are:
320	
321	- power
322	- brightness
323	- color temperature
324	- target temperature
325	- mode
326	- fan speed
327	- cover position
328	
329	Lower-level or more device-specific controls can still be extended through `control_device`, but they are no longer the default public usage model.
330	
331	## Code Structure
332	
333	The server currently has three main layers:
334	
335	- `adapter/`
336	  Talks to `mijiaAPI`, handles login, device discovery, and QR login UX
337	- `mcp_server/core/`
338	  Handles result formatting, capability processing, intent routing, and normalization
339	- `mcp_server/device_definitions/` and `mcp_server/device_resources/`
340	  Define standard capabilities, intents, and product-style resource models
341	
342	Capabilities and routing are now driven by explicit definition tables instead of plugin-style auto discovery. This makes the behavior clearer and more stable for AI clients.
343	

